  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nDew point temperatures this afternoon are only in the mid 40s to\nlower 50s across the forecast area. However, as southerly winds\nincrease overnight, a fetch of Gulf moisture will bring them up\ninto the mid 60s over west central Illinois by daybreak. Ahead\nof the incoming cold front, this juicier air mass will\noverspread central Illinois on Tuesday. As temperatures over\nmuch of the area reach well into the 90s, triple digit heat\nindices will return to areas near and south of I-72. There\nremains a concern with needing a heat advisory, but at the\npresent time, the highest dew points will be closest to the\nfront, where temperatures should only reach the 80s. With the \nfront stalling over central Illinois, highs in the 90s will\ncontinue south of it the remainder of the week. ECMWF Extreme\nForecast Index is near the top of the scale through Thursday.\nTemperatures may fall just shy of records, but will be close in\nsome instances (see table in Climate section below).\n\nMixed layer CAPE's over the forecast area are progged to reach\n2000-2500 J/kg on Tuesday, with decent 0-6km shear around 30-40\nknots immediately along the front. Several of the CAM's are showing\ndevelopment near the I-72 corridor toward mid to late afternoon,\nthough just south of the axis of highest shear. SPC Day2\nconvective outlook hasn't changed too much, still highlighting a\nlevel 1 risk of severe weather over nearly the entire forecast\narea. Periods of shower/storms continue through mid week, though\nthey will shift northward with time as the front begins to lift\nback north.\n\nAs we get into the weekend and early next week, persist upper\nlevel high pressure over the lower Mississippi Valley should\nfinally shift eastward, allowing for some cooler weather to\narrive by Monday. However, the international models are\nsuggesting ridging redeveloping over the Plains early next week,\nedging eastward. with a warming trend. CPC outlook for September\n20-24th indicates a 50-60% chance of above normal temperatures\nto start autumn.",
